WEBVTT

NOTE Module 06 transcript — auto-aligned to narration.wav (133.3s). en.

1
00:00:00.178 --> 00:00:02.315
Welcome to Module Six — object detection.

2
00:00:02.671 --> 00:00:08.976
Now we don't just classify an image, we find and label every object inside it.

3
00:00:09.366 --> 00:00:13.728
We'll cover the metrics, anchor boxes, suppression, and the YOLO family.

4
00:00:14.093 --> 00:00:18.320
Detection is classification plus localization, repeated for every object.

5
00:00:18.697 --> 00:00:27.687
The metric that grades a box is intersection over union — the overlap between prediction and ground truth, from zero to one.

6
00:00:28.091 --> 00:00:38.657
Its smarter cousins, G-I-O-U, D-I-O-U, and C-I-O-U, add penalties for distance and aspect ratio, and as a loss they converge faster than plain L2 on boxes.

7
00:00:39.007 --> 00:00:46.929
Most detectors start from anchor boxes — prior shapes tiled across the image at varied scales and ratios.

8
00:00:47.319 --> 00:00:51.665
Each anchor predicts an offset, an objectness score, and a class.

9
00:00:52.049 --> 00:00:59.999
Running k-means on your ground-truth boxes finds good priors, and stacking anchors across a feature pyramid handles multiple scales.

10
00:01:00.353 --> 00:01:04.480
Newer anchor-free detectors like FCOS and CenterNet skip this step entirely.

11
00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:12.817
Any detector produces a flood of overlapping boxes, so you clean them up with non-maximum suppression.

12
00:01:13.207 --> 00:01:19.499
Sort by confidence, keep the top box, and drop anything that overlaps it too much.

13
00:01:19.875 --> 00:01:26.990
Soft-NMS decays scores instead of deleting, and DIoU-NMS is a modern refinement — tune the threshold per dataset.

14
00:01:27.343 --> 00:01:30.450
The YOLO family made all of this real-time.

15
00:01:31.274 --> 00:01:40.010
It predicts on a grid in a single pass, with a head that emits objectness, class, and box offsets together.

16
00:01:40.395 --> 00:01:50.629
Versions three through eight trade blows on speed and accuracy, and you can export any of them to TFLite or ONNX for mobile and edge.

17
00:01:50.987 --> 00:01:54.948
To grade the whole system, use mean average precision.

18
00:01:55.304 --> 00:02:05.438
Compute the area under each class's precision–recall curve, then average across classes — at I-O-U zero-point-five for VOC, or averaged across thresholds for COCO.

19
00:02:05.803 --> 00:02:08.645
Always report it next to per-class F1.

20
00:02:09.015 --> 00:02:13.120
Let's train a YOLO detector — open the labs.
